---
/*
  One construct at six scales. Every scale is a frame drawn inside the one
  above it: a value inside a locus, a locus inside an application, an
  application inside an environment, an environment inside a fleet. Each
  frame's own beat sits at its closing edge, so reading down pulls back
  one frame at a time and the last bracket closes where the argument does.

  Every panel is a real program. scripts/check-site.mjs extracts each
  `{ title, code }` entry below (with its `prelude`, the declarations a
  panel needs but does not show) and runs `hale check` on it; the fleet
  plan is checked for referential integrity, the rule `hale fleet check`
  applies.
*/
import Hale from './Hale.astro';

const panels = [
  /*
    The units chapter's own program (docs/src/basics/math.md, "Your own
    units"), byte for byte: a value says what it counts.
  */
  { title: 'money.hl', code: `unit cent;
unit USD = 100 cent;
unit pct = 1/100;
unit bp = 1/100 pct;

type Money  = quantity Int in cent;
type Ledger = Money { round: half_even; }
type Rate   = quantity Int in bp;

fn main() {
    let fee = 1_250_000USD * 3bp;     // exactly 375 USD
    let odd = 1_234_567USD * 3bp;     // 370.3701 USD: still exact here
    let paid: Ledger = odd;           // rounded where the policy is
    let cents = odd.in(cent) or ceil; // or name the rounding yourself
    println(fee, " ", odd, " ", paid, " ", cents);
}` },
  { title: 'frontier.hl', code: `effect io = { syscall, block };

@effects(none: {io})
@budget(alloc_per_call = 0)
fn checksum(seed: Int, n: Int) -> Int {
    let mut acc = seed;
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < n {
        acc = acc * 31 + i;
        i += 1;
    }
    return acc;
}` },
  { title: 'session.hl', code: `locus Session {
    params {
        id: Int = 0;
        seen: Int = 0;
    }

    fn record() {
        self.seen += 1;
    }
}
// dissolve frees the whole region.
// No collector walks it.` },
  { title: 'nesting.hl', code: `locus Shard {
    params { slot: Int = 0; }

    contract { expose slot: Int; }
}

locus Index {
    params { width: Int = 0; }

    contract { consume slot: Int; }

    accept(s: Shard) {
        self.width += 1;
    }
}` },
  { title: 'desk.hl', code: `type Order { id: Int; desk: String; }

topic Placed { payload: Order; keyed_by desk; }
topic Filled { payload: Order; }

locus Desk {
    params { name: String = "equities"; }

    bus {
        subscribe Placed as on_order where key == self.name;
        publish Filled;
    }

    fn on_order(o: Order) {
        Filled <- o;
    }
}` },
  { title: 'main.hl', code: `locus Worker { params { valid: Int = 1; } }

main locus App {
    params {
        workers: Worker = Worker { };
    }

    placement {
        workers: pinned(cores = 0..4, replicas = 4);
    }
}
// Four single-threaded replicas, one per core.
// Not one line of Worker changed.` },
  { title: 'supervise.hl', code: `locus Worker {
    params { valid: Int = 1; }

    closure must_be_valid {
        self.valid ~~ 1 within 0;
        epoch birth;
    }
}

locus Supervisor {
    on_failure(w: Worker, err: ClosureViolation) {
        println(err.locus, " broke ", err.closure);
        quarantine (w);
    }
}` },
  /*
    `prelude` is declarations the panel deliberately does not show — the
    loci and topics these claims quantify over. scripts/check-site.mjs
    prepends it before running `hale check`, so the snippet is genuinely
    checked (the groups resolve, so the claims are not vacuous) while the
    panel stays the four interesting lines. Nothing renders it.
  */
  { title: 'claims.hl',
    prelude: `topic Tasks { payload: Int; }

locus Planner {
    bus { publish Tasks; }
    fn plan(n: Int) { Tasks <- n; }
}
locus Worker {
    bus { subscribe Tasks as take; }
    fn take(n: Int) { }
}
locus Auditor {
    fn record(n: Int) { }
}
`,
    code: `group planners = { Planner };
group workers  = { Worker };
group audit    = { Auditor };

main locus Fleet {
    claims {
        isolated:   forbid reaches(planners, audit) via { calls };
        wired:      require subscribes(some workers, topic Tasks);
        one_writer: count publishers(topic Tasks) <= 1;
    }
}` },
  { title: 'constitution.hl',
    prelude: `topic Settled { payload: Int; }

locus Billing {
    bus { publish Settled; }
    fn settle(n: Int) { Settled <- n; }
}
locus Research {
    fn study(n: Int) { }
}
locus Ledger {
    bus { subscribe Settled as post; }
    fn post(n: Int) { }
}
`,
    code: `group billing  = { Billing };
group research = { Research };

// Authored once. Every world that adopts it proves it again,
// against its own graph.
constitution Core {
    tenant_iso: forbid reaches(billing, research);
    one_writer: count publishers(topic Settled) == 1;
}

main locus App {
    params {
        b: Billing = Billing { };
        r: Research = Research { };
        l: Ledger = Ledger { };
    }

    claims { adopt Core; }
}` },
  { title: 'prod.plan.json', code: `{
  "schema": "1.1", "name": "prod",
  "instances": [
    {"id": "oms-0", "artifact": "artifacts/oms.json"},
    {"id": "gw-0",  "artifact": "artifacts/gateway.json"}
  ],
  "routes": [
    {"id": "request", "transport": "unix",
     "publishers":  [{"instance": "oms-0", "topic": "t::Order"}],
     "subscribers": [{"instance": "gw-0",  "topic": "t::Order"}]}
  ]
}` },
];
const p = Object.fromEntries(panels.map((x) => [x.title, x]));
---
<div class="scale" data-scale="fleet">
  <span class="scale-label">fleet <i>prod.plan.json</i></span>
  <div class="scale" data-scale="environment">
    <span class="scale-label">environment <i>constitution.hl</i></span>
    <div class="scale" data-scale="application">
      <span class="scale-label">application <i>claims.hl</i></span>
      <div class="scale" data-scale="locus">
        <span class="scale-label">locus <i>ownership · nesting · communication · execution · failure</i></span>
        <div class="scale" data-scale="value">
          <span class="scale-label">value <i>money.hl · frontier.hl</i></span>
          <article class="beat">
            <div class="copy">
              <h3>A value says what it counts. A function says what it may do.</h3>
              <p>
                A program declares its units and the equations between them,
                then integer types counted in them: money in cents, sizes in
                bytes, rates in basis points. Arithmetic stays exact, widening
                is free, and narrowing is written where it happens, with the
                rounding you chose. Units, quantities, identities and ranges
                arrived in 0.23.
              </p>
              <p>
                Ten effect classes describe what a function can reach: syscall,
                block, time, entropy, env, ffi, publish, spawn, recursion and
                alloc. A function can declare which it uses, plus a numeric
                budget such as zero allocations per call, and the compiler
                proves the declaration across everything the function can
                reach. A violation names the path.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
              <Hale code={p['money.hl'].code} title="money.hl" kind="value · new in 0.23" />
              <Hale code={p['frontier.hl'].code} title="frontier.hl" kind="value" />
            </div>
          </article>
        </div>

        <article class="beat">
          <div class="copy">
            <h3>Memory belongs to the locus.</h3>
            <p>
              A locus owns a region, and dissolving the locus frees the whole region. There is no collector and no lifetime annotation,
              because no pointer can cross sideways between loci.
            </p>
          </div>
          <Hale code={p['session.hl'].code} title="session.hl" kind="locus" />
        </article>

        <article class="beat">
          <div class="copy">
            <h3>Children live inside their parent.</h3>
            <p>
              A child's region is part of the region that owns it. A parent can
              call its children, and nothing can reach a locus it does not own.
              This is the one-owner rule as the compiler sees it.
            </p>
          </div>
          <Hale code={p['nesting.hl'].code} title="nesting.hl" kind="locus" />
        </article>

        <article class="beat">
          <div class="copy">
            <h3>A topic is the only way across.</h3>
            <p>
              Loci hold no references to each other. A message goes up to a
              declared topic and back down to every subscriber. Publish and
              subscribe are declarations, so the compiler sees the whole
              message graph: who publishes, who subscribes, whether the
              payloads match, and whether a handler can ever run.
            </p>
          </div>
          <Hale code={p['desk.hl'].code} title="desk.hl" kind="locus" />
        </article>

        <article class="beat">
          <div class="copy">
            <h3>Where a locus runs is decided in main.</h3>
            <p>
              A placement block maps loci onto a shared pool, a dedicated
              thread, a pinned core or a NUMA node. The loci do not mention
              threads. This example runs four single-threaded replicas without
              a change to <code>Worker</code>.
            </p>
          </div>
          <Hale code={p['main.hl'].code} title="main.hl" kind="locus" />
        </article>

        <article class="beat" id="supervision">
          <div class="copy">
            <h3>Failure goes to the owner.</h3>
            <p>
              A call that can fail says so in its type, and the caller decides
              what to do at the call site. A broken invariant in a locus goes
              to the parent that owns it, which can restart it, quarantine it,
              or pass it up. Nothing travels sideways.
            </p>
            <table class="channels">
              <thead><tr><th scope="col">what failed</th><th scope="col">declared as</th><th scope="col">handled</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><td>a call</td><td><code>fallible(E)</code> in its type</td><td>at the call site</td></tr>
                <tr><td>an invariant</td><td>a <code>closure</code> on the locus</td><td>by the parent: restart, quarantine, or pass it up</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <Hale code={p['supervise.hl'].code} title="supervise.hl" kind="locus" />
        </article>
      </div>

      <article class="beat">
        <div class="copy">
          <h3>A claim is one sentence about the whole graph.</h3>
          <p>
            Claims are declared in <code>main</code> and checked by <code>hale check</code> as
            errors. Six forms cover absence, existence, cardinality, coverage,
            boundary inventories and cost. A violation prints a minimal
            countermodel in your own names, and claims compile to no code. The
            compiler refuses to certify a call it cannot resolve, and says
            where it could not see.
          </p>
        </div>
        <Hale code={p['claims.hl'].code} title="claims.hl" kind="application" />
      </article>
    </div>

    <article class="beat">
      <div class="copy">
        <h3>A constitution is a claimset shared across programs.</h3>
        <p>
          Write the claims once. Every entry point that adopts the
          constitution proves them against its own graph. Composition is by
          union only, so an environment can add a rule and cannot weaken one.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Hale code={p['constitution.hl'].code} title="constitution.hl" kind="environment" />
    </article>
  </div>

  <article class="beat">
    <div class="copy">
      <h3>A fleet plan is checked the same way.</h3>
      <p>
        Each binary emits the topology it proved. A plan names the instances
        and routes production runs, and <code>hale fleet check</code> checks claims across
        them, so two correct binaries wired into an incorrect fleet are caught
        here. The certificate is signed over the artifact's bytes.
      </p>
    </div>
    <Hale code={p['prod.plan.json'].code} title="prod.plan.json" kind="fleet" lang="json" />
  </article>
</div>
